    "coreExplanation": [
      "## The three-part item decision",
      "For each question:",
      "### 1. Identify the task",
      "Determine what the stem asks:",
      "- Definition\n- Theory or theorist\n- Purpose of a skill\n- Best response\n- First or next action\n- Assessment priority\n- Ethical responsibility\n- Statistical interpretation",
      "### 2. Generate the governing concept",
      "Before becoming absorbed in the answer choices, identify the rule or concept that should govern the answer.",
      "Examples:",
      "- Immediate safety before routine exploration\n- Informed consent before beginning a service\n- Developmental alignment when selecting interventions\n- Client collaboration in treatment planning\n- Theory-consistent technique when a theory is named\n- Consultation or referral when competence is exceeded",
      "### 3. Compare the options against the task",
      "Eliminate options that are:",
      "- Outside the counselor’s role\n- Premature\n- Unresponsive to the stem\n- Inconsistent with the named theory\n- More intrusive than necessary\n- Based on an unsupported assumption\n- Helpful eventually but not the best first or next step",
      "## A flexible pacing model",
      "The current examination permits an average of 67.5 seconds per item. ExamPath recommends using that as a monitoring average rather than an inflexible timer.",
      "Suggested checkpoints if a learner uses the full 225 minutes evenly:",
      "A learner who wants review time must move somewhat faster than these even-pace checkpoints during the first pass. The exact plan should be tested during full-length practice exams rather than chosen for the first time on examination day.",
      "## Handling a difficult item",
      "When stuck:",
      "1. Restate the question in simpler language.\n2. Identify the domain or subject being tested.\n3. Eliminate clearly inconsistent options.\n4. Choose the most defensible remaining answer.\n5. Flag for review if the interface permits and time remains.\n6. Move forward before one item disrupts the whole examination.",
      "## Common priority patterns",
      "These are reasoning guides, not automatic rules:",
      "- Safety and urgent risk generally precede routine intervention.\n- Assessment generally precedes a treatment conclusion when critical information is missing.\n- Client understanding and informed consent precede participation in a service.\n- Collaboration is preferred when no emergency, legal mandate, or contraindication requires unilateral action.\n- The least assumptive response is often stronger than an option that diagnoses or interprets beyond the information given.\n- When a named theory appears, answer within that theory rather than choosing a favorite intervention from another approach."
    ],
